WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.640
- My first guest went through
their share of setbacks

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:05.070
to become one of the
biggest country acts today.

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:06.817
They have three Grammys, an
Academy of Country Music Awards,

00:00:06.817 --> 00:00:11.817
and even a daytime Emmy, because why not?

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.080
Their latest album is called Nightfall.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:15.740
It's amazing, you should get it.

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:18.024
Give it up for Little Big Town y'all.

00:00:18.024 --> 00:00:20.274
(applause)

00:00:22.821 --> 00:00:24.154
- [Kelly] Hello!

00:00:25.486 --> 00:00:28.980
Oh my gosh I'm so bummed that
y'all can't be here in person.

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:32.876
This sucks, but it's so
great to see all of you.

00:00:32.876 --> 00:00:33.709
- Good to see you.

00:00:33.709 --> 00:00:35.353
- Good to see you.

00:00:35.353 --> 00:00:37.910
- So did you guys hear the family harmony

00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:40.100
that just happened on the show?

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:41.918
- Oh, wow.

00:00:41.918 --> 00:00:42.751
- [Kelly] I know.

00:00:42.751 --> 00:00:43.584
- That was phenomenal.

00:00:43.584 --> 00:00:44.417
- Their voices.

00:00:44.417 --> 00:00:45.523
And all those parts that they were,

00:00:45.523 --> 00:00:47.020
it's so cool.

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:48.530
- I know that low end,

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:50.180
especially when they went down for bones,

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:51.930
I was like, I felt it in my bones.

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:53.840
It was so good.

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:58.075
So how long were you quarantined
from each other this year?

00:00:58.075 --> 00:00:59.170
(laughing)

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:00.171
He laughs.

00:01:00.171 --> 00:01:01.372
- Like.

00:01:01.372 --> 00:01:02.205
- I don't know.

00:01:02.205 --> 00:01:03.038
- Several months.

00:01:03.038 --> 00:01:04.940
- Several months, and
it's like the longest

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:07.973
that we've ever been
apart from each other.

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:12.840
So yeah, it was very emotional
when we got back together.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:14.380
- Kinda like old married people,

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:16.880
we really grew to look like each other

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.440
and we had never, ever
been apart for more than,

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:21.283
you know, a long weekend.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:25.500
- Oh man, I know that that's
been the hardest thing is just,

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:26.720
I think as creative people too,

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:29.610
we love to be inspired by
people and around people.

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:31.800
It's a very hard thing, but Kimberly,

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:33.866
you have a personal
project coming out, right?

00:01:33.866 --> 00:01:34.880
- I do.

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:39.500
I wrote a children's book about
our family's adoption story

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:43.650
on our little Dolly, and it
comes out this holiday season.

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:44.840
I'm so excited.

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:46.976
- Oh, that's such an
awesome thing because,

00:01:46.976 --> 00:01:49.470
especially, we're talking
about representation

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:50.410
and inclusion of everyone.

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:51.650
There's a lot of stories out there,

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:54.120
and they don't really
get to hear their story.

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:55.660
And that's a really cool thing for kids

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:56.570
that are adopted as well.

00:01:56.570 --> 00:01:58.400
And like, and if you've
adopted a kid, that's a,

00:01:58.400 --> 00:01:59.620
that's a beautiful project.

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:01.790
That was a great idea, and
it's why I brought it up.

00:02:01.790 --> 00:02:02.820
- Thank you.

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:04.450
It's called a Dolly for Christmas,

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:06.350
and it's just a story about hope,

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:08.010
whether you have an adopted child or not.

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:10.960
It's just about hope and
never giving up on a dream.

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:14.300
My little Daisy prayed
for a baby for five years.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:16.390
And finally she prayed,

00:02:16.390 --> 00:02:17.830
and she asked Santa Claus for one

00:02:17.830 --> 00:02:20.600
and right at Christmas time,
we got our little Dolly.

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:21.651
- Oh my God, that's so--

00:02:21.651 --> 00:02:23.261
- Y'all got me crying over here.

00:02:23.261 --> 00:02:24.621
- And Philip's crying.

00:02:24.621 --> 00:02:25.844
(laughing)

00:02:25.844 --> 00:02:27.637
- It's so great.

00:02:27.637 --> 00:02:29.870
So wait, Jimmy, you all did some

00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:31.560
online challenges in lock down,

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:34.799
so what is the hashtag
basketball beer challenge

00:02:34.799 --> 00:02:36.569
and who did it?

00:02:36.569 --> 00:02:38.400
(laughing)

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:41.190
- That we had seen that
online so many times.

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:44.000
And then we thought we
would take it on ourselves

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.540
to kind of go along with our
new song, Wine, Beer, Whiskey,

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:49.640
and up the basketball
challenge a little bit

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:51.330
'cause most of the time it was beer.

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:56.330
And so I did a beer, a canned wine,

00:02:56.890 --> 00:03:01.610
our canned wine and a bottle of Crown.

00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:05.143
That was the one that I thought,
okay, I may lose a tooth.

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:07.850
- Oh my God.

00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:09.990
So Kimberly, did you try this challenge?

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:12.759
- I mean, I was making
some biscuits that day.

00:03:12.759 --> 00:03:15.009
I didn't try the challenge.

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:17.987
- I love you so,

00:03:17.987 --> 00:03:19.300
you were making biscuits.

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:22.130
Well Philip, how did your challenge go?

00:03:22.130 --> 00:03:25.100
- Man, that is harder to do than it looks.

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:28.720
And one take, my sweet
wife was filming me,

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:32.360
and the beer went flying and
hit her right in the stomach.

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:33.650
- [Kelly] Oh my gosh.

00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:34.923
- [Kimberly] That's terrible.

00:03:38.196 --> 00:03:40.320
- Well, Karen, did you do it?

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.280
- Well, I'm super competitive,

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:46.270
and I could probably continue
to do this all night long,

00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:48.524
like tonight after we finish this,

00:03:48.524 --> 00:03:50.880
but it didn't go well for me,

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:54.710
and I pride myself in being
fairly athletic and coordinated,

00:03:54.710 --> 00:03:56.599
but, and then into the pool,

00:03:56.599 --> 00:03:58.249
- [Kelly] And then into the pool.

00:03:59.669 --> 00:04:01.600
Well Philip, last time you were here,

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:05.760
you had a song called Over
Drinking, which I love.

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:07.683
I'm sensing a theme, y'all.

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:13.623
- Hey, and you know, we
love all the alcohol.

00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:16.110
Wine, beer, whiskey.

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:17.954
- You're just being inclusive.

00:04:17.954 --> 00:04:19.864
- [Philip] That's right,
trying to include everyone.

00:04:19.864 --> 00:04:22.880
That song, particularly,
was really fun to write

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:24.370
because we were out on the road,

00:04:24.370 --> 00:04:26.450
and we were writing with Tofer
Brown and Sean McConnell,

00:04:26.450 --> 00:04:27.370
two great writers,

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:30.580
and we had finished a song in the morning

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:32.920
that was really, like,
emotional and powerful.

00:04:32.920 --> 00:04:35.570
And then I was like, man,
we need a, we need a break.

00:04:35.570 --> 00:04:37.870
We got some wine, we got
some beers and some whiskey,

00:04:37.870 --> 00:04:38.920
what do you guys want?

00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:40.370
And that's, and Tofer went off,

00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:43.090
and we started and then we
wrote this song in like an hour.

00:04:43.090 --> 00:04:43.923
It was so much fun.

00:04:43.923 --> 00:04:46.200
- It's such a great song,

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:49.050
and it's such a, it's one of
those songs that like I heard,

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:51.809
and I was like, how has
this not been done before?

00:04:51.809 --> 00:04:53.050
Like, I was like,

00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:55.000
like this is such a great idea,

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:58.080
and it's so clever and
it's such a sing along.

00:04:58.080 --> 00:04:59.220
And it's one of those that, you know,

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:00.440
when we're allowed to go to shows

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:01.690
and our lives don't suck,

00:05:03.511 --> 00:05:05.590
this is the one everybody
will want you to sing

00:05:05.590 --> 00:05:06.423
and sing along to.

00:05:06.423 --> 00:05:07.300
It's just so fun.

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:10.501
So I mean, Kim, like
last time you were also

00:05:10.501 --> 00:05:13.660
about to play at Carnegie
Hall and the Apollo

00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:15.710
for the first time, so how
did that go for everyone?

00:05:15.710 --> 00:05:17.850
- That was something
you don't even dream up.

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:19.430
It was bigger and better

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:21.930
than we could have ever, ever dreamed.

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:24.910
Yeah, we started our
tour at Carnegie Hall,

00:05:24.910 --> 00:05:27.800
and then we followed it with
two shows at the Apollo,

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:31.270
and we thought 2020 was
just like the best year ever

00:05:31.270 --> 00:05:33.738
because it had started out so amazing.

00:05:33.738 --> 00:05:34.960
And then we had a little hiccup,

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:37.540
but we'll be back at
it as quick as we can,

00:05:37.540 --> 00:05:39.810
but that start was phenomenal.

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:41.620
There's nothing that will ever beat that.

00:05:41.620 --> 00:05:42.560
- It was magical.

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:44.421
- Oh man, yeah, it looked magical.

00:05:44.421 --> 00:05:47.570
Well, Karen, I know that you love clothes.

00:05:47.570 --> 00:05:51.100
So you can add stylist
though to your resume, right?

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:55.710
- I mean, I'm the backup
stylist, you know?

00:05:55.710 --> 00:05:57.077
- You're great at clothes.

00:05:57.077 --> 00:05:59.204
- There is like dressing, you know,

00:05:59.204 --> 00:06:02.148
Ken and Barbie dolls every year or so.

00:06:02.148 --> 00:06:04.740
But yeah, I love to do it,

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:07.710
and I had to do some of the press tour,

00:06:07.710 --> 00:06:10.040
which ended up being
like, you know, Kelly,

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:14.700
like so many outfits and
so many changes of clothes,

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:17.520
and that's before COVID, so I would,

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:21.070
we would finish like doing
something, a TV performance,

00:06:21.070 --> 00:06:23.700
and I would run to Bergdorfs.

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:24.533
- Just terrible.

00:06:24.533 --> 00:06:26.750
She hates that.
- Terrible.

00:06:26.750 --> 00:06:28.170
Yeah so, but we also,

00:06:28.170 --> 00:06:30.170
we've made some big fashion mistakes.

00:06:30.170 --> 00:06:32.350
I don't know if you ever have,
but back in the early days--

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:34.090
- Oh my gosh, are you kidding?

00:06:34.090 --> 00:06:36.380
I'm like how they wore it wrong, you know,

00:06:36.380 --> 00:06:38.233
before my stylist got ahold of me.

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:42.390
Well, we actually, wait, speaking of this,

00:06:42.390 --> 00:06:45.510
we have a couple of those
early red carpet photos,

00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:47.630
and I thought we could
just take a look at some

00:06:47.630 --> 00:06:50.010
and you can tell us about them.

00:06:50.010 --> 00:06:52.050
Here's one, so what do you remember?

00:06:52.050 --> 00:06:53.696
- Oh my stars.

00:06:53.696 --> 00:06:55.010
(laughing)

00:06:55.010 --> 00:06:56.411
- [Kelly] Oh my stars.

00:06:56.411 --> 00:06:58.410
- That is terrible, terrible.

00:06:58.410 --> 00:07:00.493
- [Kelly] If terrible is awesome.

00:07:02.530 --> 00:07:04.390
- I mean, Johnny, your resident called,

00:07:04.390 --> 00:07:06.270
he wants his hair back.

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:08.320
- [kelly] No, keep it, keep it.

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:09.915
So wait, and then there's this,

00:07:09.915 --> 00:07:11.483
what do you think of this one?

00:07:11.483 --> 00:07:12.483
I love this.

00:07:14.243 --> 00:07:15.243
I love this.

00:07:17.952 --> 00:07:21.170
- The one thing that I want
back from those days are my abs.

00:07:21.170 --> 00:07:23.370
- [Kelly] Oh Lord, if there is anything

00:07:23.370 --> 00:07:25.960
I did not appreciate more in
my twenties, it was my body.

00:07:25.960 --> 00:07:27.980
It is just, wow, downhill.

00:07:27.980 --> 00:07:29.910
Just you 20-year-olds
hear this loud and clear,

00:07:29.910 --> 00:07:30.973
it's over.

00:07:32.646 --> 00:07:35.180
It's like, just, you think
you're fat right now.

00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:36.913
You're not, wait, you will be.

00:07:38.500 --> 00:07:40.260
And then you'll be like, damn.

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:43.840
Anyway, but, so, Kimberly,
so on the new song,

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:46.890
someone told me that you
guys used mouth trumpets

00:07:46.890 --> 00:07:48.630
on this song, and I can actually hear it,

00:07:48.630 --> 00:07:51.370
especially at the end,
one of the last ones.

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:53.040
So care to explain how that happened

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:55.520
and was wine, beer, or whiskey involved?

00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:57.160
(laughing)

00:07:57.160 --> 00:07:57.993
- Absolutely.

00:08:00.221 --> 00:08:02.073
(mouth trumpet noise)

00:08:02.073 --> 00:08:02.906
- Oh yes.

00:08:04.503 --> 00:08:06.870
- I think Jimmy has
the best mouth trumpet.

00:08:06.870 --> 00:08:08.683
(mouth trumpet noise)

00:08:08.683 --> 00:08:10.190
- [Kelly] Oh, I can't do that.

00:08:10.190 --> 00:08:11.323
Did y'all all do it?

00:08:12.460 --> 00:08:13.624
- Yeah.

00:08:13.624 --> 00:08:15.320
- Yes, some trumpets
are better than others.

00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:17.760
- We considered leaving it like that.

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:20.237
- You can hear the mouth
trumpet at the end though.

00:08:20.237 --> 00:08:22.480
At the very end, that's the best part,

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:23.910
and it's like, I want to go to that party,

00:08:23.910 --> 00:08:25.680
where at the end, I feel
like I'm so awesome,

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:27.430
I can play a trumpet with my mouth.

00:08:28.476 --> 00:08:30.700
- Well, we were going to
keep the mouth trumpets,

00:08:30.700 --> 00:08:34.370
but we decided to add
Jacob from the Brummies,

00:08:34.370 --> 00:08:35.370
a great little band,

00:08:35.370 --> 00:08:38.880
and he played trumpet on the record.

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:40.470
So we were like, we gotta have both.

00:08:40.470 --> 00:08:42.500
- I love it. The vibe is so cool guys.

00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:43.500
Like how the song starts out.

00:08:43.500 --> 00:08:44.590
It's like, where is this going?

00:08:44.590 --> 00:08:46.013
Like, and then how it ends.

00:08:46.013 --> 00:08:47.690
It literally is so cool.

00:08:47.690 --> 00:08:48.760
That's what I love about you,

00:08:48.760 --> 00:08:51.200
you're so adventurous and you
play with different genres,

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:53.540
and it's just really,
it's really, really cool

00:08:53.540 --> 00:08:54.373
to be a fan of you

00:08:54.373 --> 00:08:55.760
and how y'all are unafraid of that.

00:08:55.760 --> 00:08:58.460
And it's nice to go on
the ride with y'all.

